content.text:
This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:
   - I am PanelDev, a standing teammate role owning the cavecrew-mcp fleet panel (repo: /home/phili/tools/cavecrew-mcp, WSL). Chief-ordered mandate: continuously improve the fleet panel including design. Hard laws from my charter: NEVER touch/kill/bind the live :3200 process, dev/test on :3290, mock runners on :3291 for button-click testing (never fire action endpoints at live production bots 3201-3208), never push (team-lead pushes), commit small focused changes with repo-style messages, report caveman-style with exact file:line references per round.
   - Completed 7 incremental improvement rounds (idleGuard/lastDeath/dot, panic-flag, source-chip+design, event-feed, event-feed refinements, Tribe Board, Vault) — all prior to the most recent context window, all committed.
   - Delivered a PANEL V3 research dossier (`cave/PANEL_V3_SPEC.md`) covering data inventory, 4 named gaps (G1-G4), feature specs, and an architecture recommendation (3-way file split), later extended with real Minecraft item icon + durability bar research per a chief addition.
   - After team-lead ran an implementation Workflow (Mission Control, Economy, Tribe Stat Wall, the 3-way file split), I was assigned and completed an "integration round": (1) `/mc-icon/<name>.png` route + real icons everywhere, (2) heldItem hero icon + durability line in Mission Control, (3) BariBrute baritone-bot card support, (4) a one-line CSS fix (`.fel-neutral .sw-word`), (5) full QA on :3290.
   - Chief then asked directly (German) "wann wird das panel endlich implementiert" (when will it finally be implemented) — I was to answer/investigate the deployment status.
   - Chief followed up, frustrated/confused: "ich wei nicht was dummeinst einfach machen bittw?" (I don't know what you mean, just do it please) — asking me to just make the panel work, without necessarily understanding the WSL/Windows process distinction.
   - Chief later sent (mid-turn, while I was doing live QA): "mach du einfach so dass das neue panel reachable ist, kannst auch an den team lead eskalieren" (just make the new panel reachable yourself, you can also escalate to team lead).
   - Team-lead then reported they personally deployed the new code to live :3200 (pulled Windows clone to commit fa277f5, swapped process, new pid 26452), explicitly stating this "no-touch-3200" exception was theirs alone to take (with chief's clear intent) and that my own charter/laws remain unchanged. Team-lead assigned: "YOUR TASK: final live check against the REAL :3200 now (all endpoints, icons route, BariBrute card with the LIVE bot, statwall numbers vs sources) — the same QA you ran on :3290, now against production. Report PASS/findings. Then rest until chief's design pick."
   - I executed that live QA and reported PASS with one minor, non-blocking finding, then the summary request arrived.

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - Node.js ESM, zero-dependency, no-build-step doctrine (plain `.mjs`/`.js` files, no bundler, no npm installs)
   - 3-way file split architecture: `panel.mjs` (HTTP server/routing/HTML shell only), `panel-data.mjs` (all server-side data-layer functions, no req/res dependency, independently testable), `panel-client.js` (entire client script as a real static file served via `GET /panel.js`, eliminating template-literal string-escaping bugs)
   - CSS custom-property tokens (`:root` block in panel.mjs) as the "skin pass" foundation — dark-ops palette baseline, `-rgb` triplet tokens for rgba() composition
   - mtime-based caching pattern (TODO board, Vault, Economy ledger reads) — cache invalidated only when underlying file/directory mtime changes; "age" fields always computed fresh even on a cache hit (never bake a relative-time value into a cached object)
   - "Never fabricate, never guess" honesty doctrine applied throughout: `undefined` vs `null` vs real-value distinctions preserved deliberately (e.g., heldItem field absence vs empty hand vs real item); missing data sources hide sections (`hidden` attribute) rather than showing fake zeros, EXCEPT the deaths tile which never hides (0 deaths is good news)
   - Severity/state visual language reused consistently: `.card.idle`/`.card.offline`/`.card.err`/`.card.panic` classes, 3-state connectivity dot (on/warn/off), durability-bar color thresholds (green ≥30% remaining, amber 10-30%, red <10% — a DELIBERATELY different curve from the HP/FD 0-20 scale thresholds)
   - Signature-gated DOM rebuilds (invSig, evSig, toolSig, boardSig, vaultSig, economySig) to avoid unnecessary re-render/flicker on unchanged data across the 3s poll cycle
   - Baritone bot control surface: a completely separate HTTP API (BariBrute, port 3301) from mineflayer runners — port-range-based kind inference (`isBaritonePort()`, range 3301-3309) rather than requiring a named roster entry, so future bots in that range auto-detect without code changes
   - minecraft-assets npm package (already a transitive dependency via mineflayer-web-inventory) — real MC item/block textures served locally, version-pinned to server's real MC version (1.21.11)
   - Path-traversal defense (`ICON_NAME_RE = /^[a-z0-9_]+$/`) on user-influenced filesystem lookups
